\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\series bold
_table
\series default

\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

, viz.
 
\series bold
person_table
\series default
, 
\series bold
locus_table
\series default
.
 These contain raw data copied directly from the Mega2 SQLite database (although
 some of the original fields that are only needed for Mega2 are not copied).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 
\emph on
read.Mega2DB
\emph default
 function also creates other data frames containing derived information.
 The function 
\emph on
mkfam
\emph default
 is called by 
\emph on
read.Mega2DB
\emph default
 to create the 
\series bold
fam
\series default
 data frame, the equivalent of a PLINK .fam sample information file.
 It merges the rows from the 
\series bold
pedigree_table, person_table,
\emph on
 
\series default
\emph default
and 
\series bold
phenotype_table
\series default
 to make a 
\series bold
fam
\series default
 data frame.
 The 
\series bold
pedigree_table
\series default
 has a pedigree_link
\series bold
, person_table
\series default
 has a pedigree_link and person_link
\series bold
,
\emph on
 
\series default
\emph default
and 
\series bold
phenotype_table
\series default
 has a person_link field.
 These link fields are used by the R 'merge' function to assemble the data.
  Later, you may want to prune the 
\series bold
fam
\series default
 data frame to restrict the samples processed.
 For, example you might want to eliminate samples with unknown case/control
 status.
 After you prune the 
\series bold
fam
\series default
 information, the function, 
\emph on
setfam,
\emph default
 will set the 
\series bold
fam
\series default
 data frame while also appropriately pruning the 
\series bold
phenotype_table
\series default
 and 
\series bold
unified_genotype_table
\series default
 to contain only those persons left in the 
\series bold
fam
\series default
 data frame.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The
\series bold
 unified_genotype_table
\series default
 collects all the raw byte vectors from the genotype_table stored in the
 input SQLite database by chromosome for each person and concatenates them
 into a new vector for each person.
 The
\series bold
 
\series default
concatenation can introduce up to three 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

virtual markers
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 (as mentioned above) at the end of each chromosome if the number of markers
 contained is not an exact multiple of 4 (genotypes per byte).
 The 
\series bold
marker_table
\series default
 contains the offset of the marker genotype data assuming all the markers
 across all the chromosomes are contiguous.
 There is an analogous offset column needed for the 
\series bold
unified_genotype_table
\series default
 accounting for the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

virtual markers
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 in the gaps; this offset column is computed and added to the 
\series bold
marker_table
\series default
.
 The 
\series bold
markers
\series default
\emph on
 
\emph default
data frame is a subset of the 
\series bold
marker_table
\series default
 data containing only the name, chromosome, position, original 
\series bold
marker_table
\series default
 offset and marker offset into the 
\series bold
unified_genotype_table
\series default
 vector.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Mega2R Marker Range Functions
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:mega2-Package-Marker"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We might have genotype data for thousands of samples and millions of markers
 in a study, but we are unlikely to have the memory to support the associated
 genotype matrix in R.
 The Mega2 genotype data is compressed in the SQLite database and remains
 compressed in its data frame.
 Most of the time, we do not need to decompress all of it at once.
 We typically want to examine the markers on a specific chromosome or in
 the region of some genes of interest.
 We only decompress the set of markers we need.
 The key idea is we want to find the set of markers that lie within some
 base pair range and process just those markers and then we repeat this
 for the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

next
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 range.
 We provide two ways to specify these ranges.
 In addition, we want to iterate through multiple ranges as would appear
 in a set of selected gene transcripts and process the markers.
 Finally, we process each range of markers by invoking a callback function
 on the range, markers and related data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
The 
\emph on
setRanges
\emph default
 and 
\emph on
applyFnToRanges
\emph default
 functions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One of Mega2R's strengths is its ability, via the 
\emph on
applyFnToRanges
\emph default
 and 
\emph on
applyFnToGenes
\emph default
 functions, to loop over a list of ranges or genes, for each one selecting
 markers that lie within the region/gene of interest.
 To do this, one has to have well-defined gene boundaries.
 By default, Mega2R provides an internal list of the chromosome and base
 pair ranges for the gene transcripts defined in the refGene table from
 the UCSC Genome Browser reference assembly GRCH37.
 The list was modified to eliminate multiple records of the same gene with
 the exact same transcript start and transcript end.
 The list contains about 29,000 records.
 But there are several reasons that the default transcript ranges may not
 be to your liking.
 Sometimes the transcripts for a gene appear to have overlapping start/end
 positions.
 The transcripts have one start and one end but may not account for non
 coding regions, promoters, etc.
 Perhaps you would prefer to specify transcript names and for each name
 provide a start position, end position and chromosome.
 The 
\emph on
setRanges
\emph default
 function lets you store custom ranges; it has two arguments: a range data
 frame that specifies at least the chromosome, the start position, end position
 (and optionally a name) of each range, and an index vector of three integers
 that indicates which columns in the range data frame contain the chromosome,
 start position and end position.
 If the range data frame also contains a name column, the index vector should
 have a final fourth integer that indicates the column containing the name.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 
\emph on
applyFnToRanges
\emph default
 function (and also 
\emph on
applyFnToGenes
\emph default
 function) take an initial argument that is a callback function that is
 called repeatedly for each transcript that has markers present.
 They also take a final argument that is an 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

R environment
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.

\emph on
 applyFnToRanges,
\emph default
 with no additional arguments, will iterate through each row of the default
 Mega2R ranges, determine the markers contained between the start and end
 and then invoke a callback function.
 Alternatively, 
\emph on
applyFnToRanges
\emph default
 may be given a range argument and a selector argument which will cause
 it to use the ranges supplied rather than the default.
 These two arguments are the same format as the corresponding arguments
 provided to 
\emph on
setRanges
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 
\emph on
applyFnToRanges
\emph default
 function requires a callback function of three arguments: the first is
 a data frame of the markers in the transcript, the second is a single row
 from the data frame that defines the range/transcript (minimally it has
 a name, ID, start position, end position, and chromosome), and the third
 argument is the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

R environment
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 that contains all the Mega2R data frames.
 The callback function can apply whatever analysis is required and it can
 access the other data frames available in the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

R environment
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 and even store intermediate results back into the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

R environment
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 Typically, the callback function will call either 
\emph on
getgenotypes
\emph default
 or 
\emph on
getgenotypesraw
\emph default
 to get a matrix of genotypes values for a group of markers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
The 
\emph on
setAnnotations and applyFnToGenes
\emph default
 functions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Mega2R can also load a list of genes and their transcripts from the Bioconductor
 data annotations: "org.Hs.eg.db"and "TxDb.Hsapiens.UCSC.hg19.knownGene".
 The former translates a gene name or alias to the entrez ID of the gene.
 The latter gives start position, end position, and chromosome for each
 transcript known for every gene.
 You might want to use the transcript data from another build that is available
 in Bioconductor or follow their instructions to make your own mapping.
 The Mega2R function, 
\emph on
setAnnotations
\emph default
, lets you use alternate tables.
 Mega2R will load the selected tables when you access the 
\emph on
applyFnToGenes
\emph default
 function.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 
\emph on
applyFnToGenes
\emph default
 function specifies a list of genes (via the genes_arg argument) from which
 to extract the transcripts and the corresponding ranges (If the special
 gene name, 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

*
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

, is passed as the only gene argument, the ranges for all transcripts will
 be chosen).
 Alternatively,
\emph on
 applyFnToGenes
\emph default
 supports other ways to indicate transcript ranges: on sub-ranges of chromosomes
 (via the ranges_arg argument), on the entire chromosome (via the chrs_arg
 argument), or on an arbitrary list of markers (via the markers_args argument).
 The callback function of three arguments (markers, range, environment)
 and a list of ranges is passed to 
\emph on
applyFnToRanges
\emph default
 and processed as described above.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
The 
\emph on
applyFnToMarkers
\emph default
 function
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The
\emph on
 applyFnToMarkers
\emph default
 function is the simplest function that uses the aforementioned callback
 function.
 It takes one argument that contains selected rows of the 
\series bold
marker
\series default
 data frame.
 It then invokes the callback function with the marker argument data frame,
 a NULL range, and the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

R environment
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 (The range is NULL because there is no range information; this implies
 that the callback function for 
\emph on
applyFnToMarkers 
\emph default
must check for a range value of NULL).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
The 
\emph on
getgenotypes
\emph default
 and 
\emph on
getgenotypesraw
\emph default
 functions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The functions 
\emph on
getgenotypes
\emph default
 and 
\emph on
getgenotypesraw
\emph default
 return a matrix of nucleotide pairs or a matrix of encoded integers with
 a column for each marker and containing a row per sample.
 Both functions take two arguments: rows of the 
\series bold
marker
\series default
 data frame and an environment.
 The first argument is usually computed by one of the 
\emph on
applyFnToRanges
\emph default
, 
\emph on
applyFnToGenes,
\emph default
 or 
\emph on
applyFnToMarkers
\emph default
 functions.
 The
\series bold
 marker 
\series default
data frame has two offsets that are used internally by the decompression
 code; the 
\series bold
marker
\series default
 also has a name, chromosome number and position that can be used to identify
 a marker to the user.
 The two 
\emph on
getgenotypes
\emph default
 functions are dispatches that call Rcpp code.
 The R code collects genotype and allele data from the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

R environment
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 data frames and passes these data arguments to the Rcpp code; it also calls
 the proper function for the level of compression: 2 bits or 2 bytes per
 marker.
 For each marker specified, 
\emph on
getgenotypes
\emph default
 returns for each sample a string of the corresponding pair of nucleotides,
 while 
\emph on
getgenotypesraw
\emph default
 retruns for each sample a single integer with the integer for the first
 allele shifted 16 bits and or'ed to the integer for the second allele.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
The 
\emph on
Mega2pedgene
\emph default
 Function 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:mega2pedgene-Package-Functions"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We now give a brief overview of the several processing functions that Mega2R
 makes available and that illustrate how to build new functions using Mega2R.
 A much more detailed example may be found in the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Use Case
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 section.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 'pedgene' R package (
\begin_inset Flex URL
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

https://CRAN.R-project.org/package=pedgene
\end_layout

\end_inset

) performs gene-level association tests with disease status for pedigree
 data 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "pedgene"

\end_inset

.
 Mega2R enhances 'pedgene' via the
\emph on
 Mega2pedgene
\emph default
 function, which automates and eases computation of the 'pedgene' statistics
 for all the genes in the genome.
 The 
\emph on
Mega2pedgene
\emph default
 function does this by invoking 
\emph on
applyFnToRanges
\emph default
 on the specified ranges or calls 
\emph on
applyFnToGenes
\emph default
 on gene names.
 Before running the
\emph on
 Mega2pedgene
\emph default
 function, we first call 
\emph on
init_pedgene
\emph default
 rather than 
\emph on
read.Mega2DB
\emph default
; the former uses the function in the Mega2R package to load the Mega2 database
 (
\emph on
dbmega2_import
\emph default
).
 Then it generates a modified 
\series bold
fam
\series default
 data frame, a kind of family file where the case/control values of 0/1/2
 are translated to NA/0/1, as required by 
\shape italic
pedgene
\shape default
.
 The 
\emph on
pedgene
\emph default
 package requires recoding of the genotypes 1/1, 1/2 (and 2/1), and 2/2
 (with raw encodings: 65537, 65538 (and 131073), 131074) to 0, 1, and 2
 respectively; plus the alleles must be flipped as needed so that 1 is the
 major allele.
 The callback function, 
\emph on
DOpedgene
\emph default
, transforms the raw genotype matrix and then it calls the 
\emph on
pedgene
\emph default
 function with several different marker weights (e.g., unweighted, Madsen-Browning
 weights, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 density weights).
 It returns the p-value of the kernel and burden association statistics
 for each weight.
 The results are appended to a data frame in the environment, pedgene_results.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
The 
\emph on
Mega2SKAT
\emph default
 Function 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Mega2SKAT-Function"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If one has a sample of unrelated individuals, the 
\emph on
Mega2SKAT
\emph default
 function eases the process of applying the region-based association tests
 that are implemented in the SKAT 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "SKATc,SKATa,SKATb"

\end_inset

 package.
 The SKAT package uses kernel regression approaches to compute association
 statistics such as the Sequence Kernel Association Test (SKAT) and its
 optimized version (SKAT-O), both for quantitative and dichotomous traits.
 The 
\emph on
Mega2SKAT
\emph default
 function is rather similar to 
\emph on
Mega2pedgene
\emph default
 (above).
 
\emph on
init_SKAT
\emph default
 behaves very much like 
\emph on
init_pedgene;
\emph default
 in addition, it computes a phenotype data frame.
 The Mega2SKAT function has the signature:
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Mega2SKAT = function (f, ty, gs = 1:100, genes = NULL, skat = SKAT::SKAT,
 envir = ENV, ...) 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The parameters
\series bold
 f
\series default
 and 
\series bold
ty
\series default
 specify the formula and phenotype type that are used to invoke 
\emph on
SKAT_Null_model
\emph default
.
 
\series bold
skat
\series default
 indicates the particular SKAT package function to call and the 
\series bold
...

\series default
 arguments are place holders for any additional arguments that the chosen
 
\series bold
skat
\series default
 function needs.
\end_layout

