\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace defskip
\end_inset

The code in each function illustrates how to assemble the corresponding
 data from the Mega2R data frames.
 The 
\emph on
mkphenotype
\emph default
 function is a bit subtle.
 The database contains a raw vector of 8 bytes times the number of phenotypes
 (per person).
 The 8 bytes encode either one double precision float number or two integers
 depending on the encoding of the trait phenotype in the 
\series bold
locus_table
\series default
 (quantitative or affection status).
 The 
\emph on
readBin
\emph default
 function is used to convert the raw bytes in each 8 byte vector to the
 correct form.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Another interesting aspect of the code design is how we compute genotype
 data for a large numbers of markers without needing large amount of memory.
 We build the .
\series bold
vcf
\series default
 file a chunk at a time, where a chunk contains a relatively small number
 of markers (currently 1,000).
 We generate the genotype matrix for a chunk then append the results for
 the chunk (of markers) to the .vcf file.
 Then we get a new chunk and repeat.
 This looping strategy might prove useful for other situations where the
 results can be written incrementally, a block of markers at a time.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
The 
\emph on
Mega2GenABEL
\emph default
 Function 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:mega2genabel-Package-Functions"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 'GenABEL' 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "genabel"

\end_inset

 package is available in the archive section of CRAN (
\begin_inset Flex URL
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

https://CRAN.R-project.org/package=GenABEL
\end_layout

\end_inset

).
 The GenABEL  package provides many functions for GWAS analysis and it accepts
 GWAS data in several formats.
 But Mega2 accepts input in still more formats, notably VCF, PLINK bed,
 IMPUTE2 and even Linkage.
 Thus 
\emph on
Mega2GenABEL
\emph default
 can be a bridge to easily convert data for GenABEL analysis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Before running the 
\emph on
Mega2GenABEL
\emph default
 function, you must call the 
\emph on
read.Mega2DB 
\emph default
function from the Mega2R package to load your database and you should save
 the returned 'environment' into 'ENV.' The function, 
\emph on
Mega2GenABEL
\emph default
, returns a gwaa.data-class object for the selected markers.
 It operates by re-writing the Mega2R data frames into one of the input
 formats that GenABEL supports; currently, this is PLINK .tped format.
 This requires a .tped file, a .fam file, and a .phe file; they are stored
 in a temporary directory.
 The .tped file looks very much like a VCF .vcf file with entries in each
 marker's row for the samples' genotype data.
 (But, in comparison to the .vcf file, there are fewer fields of metadata
 in a .tped file and the genotype data specify real allele labels rather
 than VCF's REF/ALT field references.) 
\emph on
Mega2GenABEL
\emph default
 calls the GenABEL 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

glue
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 functions,
\emph on
 convert.snp.tped
\emph default
, to process the a .tped file and .fam file into a generic GenABEL 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

raw
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 format file, then it converts that raw format file and a phenotype file
 into a GenABEL gwaa.data-class object via the function, 
\emph on
load.gwaa.data
\emph default
, and then it deletes the temporary files.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The function 
\emph on
Mega2ENVGenABEL
\emph default
 produces the same object as 
\emph on
Mega2GenABEL
\emph default
.
 It does not write out files, but rather converts the Mega2R genotype and
 related data in memory to a gwaa.data-class object.
 It is mainly written in Rcpp and runs 10 to 20 times faster than the 
\emph on
Mega2GenABEL
\emph default
 function.
 
\end_layout

