\begin_layout Subsection
Using Mega2R to access and process genetic data
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Creating a Mega2 database
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We have provided files in this package that contain the data from the simulation.
 These files are in PLINK ped format data:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Mega2r.ped
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Mega2r.map
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If you do not wish to install Mega2 right now, you can skip this database
 creation step and instead later use the seqsimr.db database that was placed
 in your directory by the 
\shape italic
dump_mega2rtutorial_data(".")
\shape default
 command.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
You can obtain the Mega2 program from 
\begin_inset Flex URL
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

https://watson.hgen.pitt.edu/register/
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 Then, you will invoke Mega2 on your data.
 To make matters simple, we will use a pre-constructed Mega2 batch file
 to automate the processing by Mega2.
 To run Mega2 to process and create the 'SQLite' database 'seqsimr.db', we
 issue this command at the Unix prompt from within the directory containing
 the Use Case example files:
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
mega2 MEGA2.BATCH.seqsimr
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note: The vignette associated with the Mega2R  package illustrates what
 the results of this Mega2 run would look like.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If you do not provide the command-line argument giving the name of a BATCH
 file, Mega2 will proceed to ask a series of interactive questions to collect
 the information needed to produce a database.
 In addition, it will create a Mega2.BATCH file, similar to the one we suggested
 you use.
 You can look at the "Quick Start" section of the Mega2 documentation 
\begin_inset Flex URL
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

https://watson.hgen.pitt.edu/docs/mega2_html/mega2.html
\end_layout

\end_inset

 to better understand the interactive process.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The MEGA2.BATCH.seqsimr file begins with a rather long comment section indicating
 the keyword values that may be set and their default values.
 Towards the end of the file, we set the inputs to 
\series bold
Mega2r.ped
\series default
 and 
\series bold
Mega2r.map
\series default
, indicate the input is PLINK ped format with parameters, and indicate that
 Mega2 should produce a database called 
\series bold
seqsimr.db
\series default
, etc.
 (The initial comment section is not shown and unimportant lines are elided.)
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quotation
Input_Database_Mode=1   
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quotation
Input_Format_Type=4   
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quotation
Input_Pedigree_File=Mega2r.ped   
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quotation
Input_PLINK_Map_File=Mega2r.map   
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quotation
...
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quotation
PLINK_Args= --cM --missing-phenotype -9 --trait default  
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quotation
...
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quotation
Value_Marker_Compression=1   
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quotation
Analysis_Option=Dump   
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quotation
...
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quotation
DBfile_name=seqsimr.db   
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quotation
...
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If you wish to use any of the Mega2R functions described here on your own
 data, you will have to first run 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

mega2
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 to convert your data into an 'SQLite' database.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Reading and Examining a Mega2 Database
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Mega2R package facilitates reading genetic data from a Mega2-created
 'SQLite' database.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Reading a Mega2 database
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
After you have created the SQLite database "seqsimr.db", start up R, load
 the Mega2R package, then use the function 
\emph on
read.Mega2DB
\emph default
 to read a Mega2 database.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<seqsimrdb,eval=FALSE>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

library(Mega2R)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

ENV = read.Mega2DB("seqsimr.db", verbose = FALSE)
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The first argument should be the path to the database.
 If 'verbose' is TRUE, for each data frame created in 'ENV', 
\emph on
read.Mega2DB
\emph default
 emits two lines: one with the number of rows and number of columns of the
 data frame and the other with the column names of the data frame.
 Finally, an 'environment', which contains the data frames, is returned.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<seqsimrdb2,echo=FALSE>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

ENV = read.Mega2DB("seqsimr.db")
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Verbose Flag
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When verbose is set in the initial read.Mega2DB, the value will be remembered.
 It may be used by any subsequent function.
 If verbose is TRUE, Mega2R functions will print diagnostic information.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Use of an Environment
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\color black
The environment returned is used to store the data frames that contain the
 'SQLite' database.
 The code above stores the environment in global variable 'ENV'.
 If the named argument, 'envir', is not provided in any subsequent Mega2R
 function call, R will look up the value of 'ENV' starting at the calling
 frame and chaining up the call stack to the global environment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Back to more examples.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 'ls(ENV)' function will show you all the variables in the 'ENV' environment.
 (You probably have used it without arguments to show you the variables
 in the global environment .GlobalEnv.) Type:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<ls>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

ls(ENV)
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A more informative overview of the database can be had with:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<showMENV>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

showMega2ENV() 
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Use standard R operations to examine the created data frames 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Try typing:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<str>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

str(ENV$locus_table)
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Iterating through Gene Transcripts
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Mega2R provides two ways to compute a function on the genotypes (or markers)
 in each of the transcripts.
 These are further illustrated via examples in section about 'pedgene' below.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
setRanges 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "par:applyFnToRanges"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The default ranges contains 29,062 records taken from the UCSC Genome Browser
 reference assembly GRCH37.
 We show a bit of the data frame below.
 Each row contains 5 values: a transcript id, the gene id and three position
 values: chromosome, start base pair and end base pair.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<setrange0>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

 dim(ENV$refRanges)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

 head(ENV$refRanges, 3)
\end_layout

\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
You may load your own range set instead of the default.
 You create a data frame that lists, for each range, the chromosome, the
 start position, and the end position.
 And you create an integer index vector that indicates which column contains
 which data.
 These two become the arguments to the 
\emph on
setRanges
\emph default
 function as shown in the example below.
 When the index vector contains only three entries, a range name is generated
 by concatenating the position information and adding it to the range data
 frame.
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<setrange1>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

 ranges = matrix(c(1, 2240000, 2245000,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

                   1, 2245000, 2250000,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

                   1, 3760000, 3761000),
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

                   ncol = 3, nrow = 3, byrow = TRUE)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

 setRanges(ranges, 1:3)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

 dim(ENV$refRanges)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

 head(ENV$refRanges)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If you provide an index vector with four entries, the fourth one indicates
 the column containing the names of the ranges.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<setrange2>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

 ranges = matrix(c(1, 2240000, 2245000,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

                   1, 2245000, 2250000,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

                   1, 3760000, 3761000,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

                   1, 3761000, 3762000),
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

                   ncol = 3, nrow = 4, byrow = TRUE)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

 ranges = data.frame(ranges)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

 ranges$name = LETTERS[1:4]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

 names(ranges) = c("chr", "start", "end", "name")
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

 setRanges(ranges, 1:4)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

 dim(ENV$refRanges)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

 head(ENV$refRanges)
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
applyFnToRanges
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The function, 
\emph on
applyFnToRanges,
\emph default
 goes through each range and finds the markers that fall within the bounds.
 The first argument of the 
\emph on
applyFnToRanges
\emph default
 function is a callback function with three arguments:  the markers in range,
 the selected range entry and the environment.
 The callback is invoked repeatedly for each transcript range that contains
 any markers.
 If there are no markers contained and the 'verbose' flag is set, a warning
 will be printed.
 The callback function builds a genotype matrix for the samples and each
 marker in the range.
 If necessary, the environment ('envir') can be used to store information
 between successive invocations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the examples that follow, we use 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

show
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 as the callback function, which simply prints, for each range, the range
 itself, the first three lines of the markers within the range, and the
 first three lines of the genotype matrix, in that order.
 It also prints a little banner before each argument.
 Finally, it does not print the environment argument value; it does not
 change.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<callback>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

   show = function(m, r, e) {
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

       print("** Ranges **")
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

       print(r)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

       print("** Markers **")
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

       print(head(m,3))
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

       print("** Genotypes **")
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

       print(head(getgenotypes(m),3))
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

   }
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A simple example is shown below using the ranges value that were most recently
 set.
 We see that the range named 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

A
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 contains markers in our example data set.
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<applyfntoranges1,eval=TRUE>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

    ENV$verbose = FALSE
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

    applyFnToRanges(show)
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
applyFnToRanges
\emph default
 can also be provided explicit ranges by using the 'ranges_arg' argument:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<applyfntoranges2,eval=FALSE>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

    applyFnToRanges(show, 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

                    ranges_arg =
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

                    matrix(c(1, 4000000, 5000000, "range4m",
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

                             1, 5000000, 6000000, "range5m",
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

                             1, 6000000, 7000000, "range6m",
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

                             1, 7000000, 8000000, "range7m",
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

                             1, 8000000, 9000000, "range8m",
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

                             1, 9000000,10000000, "range9m"),
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

                            ncol = 4, nrow = 6, byrow = TRUE),
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

                    indices_arg = 1:4)
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
setAnnotations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If you are iterating/selecting via genes, the default transcript database
 is "TxDb.Hsapiens.UCSC.hg19.knownGene" from Bioconductor; it is stored in the
 environment as shown below:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<setannotations0, results="hold">>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

 ENV$txdb
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

 ENV$entrezGene
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Of course, you can change this database.
 Suppose we want to use build 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

hg18
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

, we would run:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<setannotations, results="hold">>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

 setAnnotations("TxDb.Hsapiens.UCSC.hg18.knownGene", "org.Hs.eg.db")
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

 ENV$txdb
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

 ENV$entrezGene
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
applyFnToGenes 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The function
\emph on
 applyFnToGenes
\emph default
 can apply the 'show' function to the transcripts of a particular gene via
 the genes_arg argument.
 For example, here we apply the 'show' function to transcripts of the 
\shape italic
CEP104
\shape default
 gene: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<applyfntogenes19,eval=TRUE>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

    # Switch back to using build hg19
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

    setAnnotations("TxDb.Hsapiens.UCSC.hg19.knownGene", "org.Hs.eg.db")
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

    applyFnToGenes(show, genes_arg = c("CEP104"))
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 
\emph on
applyFnToGenes
\emph default
 function has several other optional arguments that can request complete
 chromosomes, (multiple) ranges of base pairs on chromosomes, or collections
 of markers.
 All these arguments define ranges that are passed to 
\emph on
applyFnToRanges
\emph default
 for evaluation.
 Note: If the 
\emph on
genes_arg
\emph default
 argument is set to the special "gene" string "*", then all transcripts
 in the Bioconductor database, will be processed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<applyfntogenes,eval=TRUE>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

    applyFnToGenes(show, ranges_arg = 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

                            matrix(c(1,  5000000, 10000000,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

                                     1, 10000000, 15000000),
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

                                    ncol = 3, nrow = 2, byrow = TRUE))
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

    # apply function "show" to all genotypes at the 'snp1' marker 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

    # NOTE: Since we are using an arbitrary collection of markers, the
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

    # range is not available.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

    applyFnToGenes(show, markers_arg = "snp1")
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

    # apply function "show" to all genotypes on chromosomes 24 and 26
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

    # remember our example database is only chr 1
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

    applyFnToGenes(show, chrs_arg=c(24, 26))
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Getting the Genotypes 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Iterating-through-the"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Encoded Genotypes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The heart of the above two functions is the calculation of the genotype
 matrix of the samples.
 We will build the matrix for the first 10 markers.
 Remember our database has 1,380 samples so we will just show the 
\emph on
head
\emph default
 of the matrix.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<getgenotypes,eval=TRUE>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

 genotype = getgenotypes(ENV$markers[1:10,])
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

 dim(genotype)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

 head(genotype, 3)
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Raw Genotypes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The function 
\emph on
getgenotypesraw
\emph default
 will be called with the same 10 markers as above.
 Recall it returns an integer encoding for each genotype.
 The integer's high 16 bits are the index for allele 1 and the low 16 bits
 are the index for allele 2.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<getgenotypesraw,eval=TRUE>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

# two ints in upper/lower half integer representing allele 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

 raw = getgenotypesraw(ENV$markers[1:10,])
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

 dim(raw)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

 head(raw, 3)
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<showraw>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

raw.allele.representation = c("0x10001", "0x10002", "0x20001", "0x20002")
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

print(as.numeric(raw.allele.representation))
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Using Mega2R to carry out automated gene-based association tests using 'pedgene'
 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:pedgene"

\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Mega2R provides functions that permit one to run the 'pedgene' function
 by Schaid et al 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "pedgene"

\end_inset

 to carry out gene-based association tests on 
\color black
family data looping over selected transcripts.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Loading a Mega2 database with 
\emph on
init_pedgene
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Rather than read the Mega2 SQLite database with the 
\emph on
read.Mega2DB
\emph default
 function as described previously, here we use a specialized 
\emph on
init_pedgene
\emph default
 function to read the Mega2 database.
 This latter function calls a utility function also used by 
\emph on
read.Mega2DB
\emph default
.
 Then it creates, edits, and rewrites the family (.fam) data, storing it
 in a 'pedgene'-compatible data frame, 
\series bold
fam
\series default
.
 (
\series bold
fam
\series default
 merges data from the 
\series bold
pedigree_table
\series default
, 
\series bold
person_table
\series default
 and 
\series bold
phenotype_table
\series default
.) (When 
\series bold
fam
\series default
 is updated, filtering is automatically done to the persons in the 
\series bold
phenotype_table
\series default
 and the 
\series bold
unified_genotype_table
\series default
 to remove any persons removed from 
\series bold
fam
\series default
.) Finally, 
\emph on
init_pedgene
\emph default
 calculates some values that will be used repeatedly and stores them in
 the environment that is returned.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<init_pedgene,message=FALSE>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

ENV = init_pedgene("seqsimr.db")
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Gene Ranges reminder 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Gene-Ranges-reminder"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Mega2R has an internal default list of the chromosome and base pair ranges
 for a number of gene transcripts.
 These transcripts come from the UCSC Genome Browser reference assembly
 GRCH37.
 The list was further modified to eliminate multiple records of the same
 gene with the exact same transcript start and transcript end.
 These data contain about 29,000 records.
 You may use the 
\shape italic
setRanges
\shape default
 function to load your own range set instead of the default, as described
 above.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Gene transcripts reminder 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Gene-transcripts-reminder"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By default, the 
\shape italic
init_pedgene
\shape default
 function sets the transcript database to 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

TxDb.Hsapiens.UCSC.hg19.knownGene
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 and the Entrez gene name mapping database to 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

org.Hs.eg.db
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 If you wish to use different databases to select transcripts by gene name,
 you must use the 
\shape italic
setAnnotations
\shape default
 function to load them from Bioconductor, as discussed above.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Running pedgene on ranges
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By default, the function 
\emph on
Mega2pedgene
\emph default
 examines the first 100 transcripts and prints the results.
 (Internally, it calls the function 
\emph on
applyFnToRanges
\emph default
.) For the seqsimr.db database, the first 100 transcripts contain only one
 transcript with several markers.
 To make this Use Case run faster, we noticed that the identified transcript
 appeared at transcript 54; so we will restrict 
\emph on
Mega2pedgene
\emph default
 to a small range of transcripts around 54, viz.
 53 through 55.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note: 'verbose' needs to be TRUE, for the diagnostics to be printed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<Mega2pedgene>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

ENV$verbose=TRUE
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

Mega2pedgene(gs=53:55)
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
You will see some reports of "No markers in range", because the database
 only contains markers on a sub range of chromosome 1 whereas the transcripts
 span the entire genome.
 Then you will see a listing of a gene name, a marker name, and count of
 the 1/1, 1/2, and 2/2 genotypes (where '1' is the major allele), e.g.,.
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
CEP104 snp24037 520 646 214 
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
...
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The markers, the range used, and the environment are passed to the callback
 function 
\emph on
DOpedgene
\emph default
.
 
\emph on
DOpedgene
\emph default
 converts the raw genotype representation returned by 
\shape italic
getgenotypesraw
\shape default
 to the values 0, 1 and 2.
 Then it runs 'pedgene'.
 The results are automatically stored in a data frame with columns: chromosome,
 gene, number of markers and base pair range followed by 'pedgene' data:
 kernel and burden, value and p-values, four values for each of three weightings
 of the markers.
 These data are saved in the data frame, 'pedgene_results', in the environment.
 They are also printed when 'verbose' is TRUE.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note: The results are always appended to the 'pedgene_results' data frame.
 You should truncate it when necessary.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
You could run 
\emph on
Mega2pedgene
\emph default
 on all the transcript entries, but it takes a rather long time.
 You would type:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<FnToRanges,eval=FALSE>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

Mega2pedgene(gs=1:nrow(ENV$refRanges), envir = ENV)
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If you run the above test, you will see that genes DISP1 and KIF26B have
 at least one p-value less than 0.01 and AK5 and STL7 at least one less than
 0.03.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Running pedgene on selected genes 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Running-pedgene-on"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
You may try searching for transcripts of specific genes.
 Here, the default transcript database is "TxDb.Hsapiens.UCSC.hg19.knownGene"
 from Bioconductor.
 Of course you can change it, if you install a new database from Bioconductor,
 as shown earlier.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We leave the command below as an exercise, as it runs a bit slowly.
 It needs to find all the transcripts for each gene, then to find all the
 markers between each pair of transcript start/end ranges, then to compute
 the genotype matrix for these markers, and finally to call the callback
 function with the appropriate arguments.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<applyF2G2,eval=FALSE>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

applyFnToGenes(DOpedgene, genes_arg = c('DISP1', 'KIF26B', 'AK5', 'ST7L'),
 envir = ENV)
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
But let us run this function for a few genes:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<applyFnToGenes,eval=TRUE,messages=FALSE>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

# turn off output & reset pedgene_results data frame
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

ENV$verbose = FALSE
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

ENV$pedgene_results = ENV$pedgene_results[0, ]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

applyFnToGenes(DOpedgene, genes_arg = c('DISP1', 'AK5'), envir = ENV)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

print(ENV$pedgene_results)
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Summary 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As with all software projects, 
\series bold
Mega2R
\series default
 is a work in progress, and so there are a number of possible improvements
 that could be made in the future.
 These include:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
defer constructing the 
\series bold
unified_genotype_table
\series default
 until the set of chromosomes that are needed can be computed and then only
 read the required chromosome genotype vectors.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
analogously to Mega2SKAT, add support for the R famSKATRC package 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Saad:2014aa,famSKATRC"

\end_inset

.
 famSKAT_RC is another package for family or unrelated gene-based association
 analysis for a mix of rare variants and common variants.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
analogously to Mega2GenABEL, add support for the CoreArray data storage
 system (e.g.
 Genomic Data Structure (GDS) format 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Zheng:2012aa"

\end_inset

).
 This will support conversion to GDS format in both the SNPRelate and the
 SeqArray data representations 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Zheng:2017aa"

\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
apply more aggressive compression of the database.
 For one, the genotype raw vector can be further compressed by gzip or a
 similar compression protocol.
 Additionally, since C does not have a way to represent missing data, we
 use -99.99 to represent it which is stored in the database as a floating
 point number (8 bytes).
 But the database (via NULL) and R (via NA) can efficiently represent a
 missing values, so we will will convert C missing (-99.99) to SQLite (NULL).
\end_layout

